# Speak the app’s language

Comments and variable names are notes for the next editor. They are not the
vocabulary to teach Mark. If a comment uses a nickname, translate it. Write new
comments in the words already on the screen.

Public words (About, labels, this table) win. Market English is fine when the
screen is silent. Do not import implementer slang into chat.

| Say | Do not say | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| light, word, needle | dial, paint job | Regime level vs an economic line |
| chevron, the turn, lookback, 1w/2w/1m | clock crawls the mark | Momentum over the chosen window |
| in / mixed / out of favor | stance (in chat) | Asset-class call |
| checklist, conditions | doors, in-doors, out-doors | Rules that set that call |
| duration risk, credit risk | | The two risks behind the strip |

About may say “the clock” for 1w/2w/1m. That is the lookback. `.about-door`
is a layout class on the About page, not a scoring term.

```js
// BAD — teaches a private dialect
// Doors own the word. The clock may crawl the mark.

// GOOD — same fact, screen language
// The checklist sets in / mixed / out. The lookback may slide the needle
// but cannot flip the call.
```
